{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-82912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-82912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2}{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{a}_{1}=2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-3)^{2}+(3-3)^{2}+(-5-(-3))^{2}+(2-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-82912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97b5954bff8b5541ae3978022ac0c3d32b2258d67c2a190b4d128a4cfbbe4760"} {"question" "q-82912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-82912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{6}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-82912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-82952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-82952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{5\\cdot 3+5\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-2)+3\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{5\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-2)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x}{‖x‖\\,‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 5+5\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{63}\\sqrt{27}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{3\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{7})≈{49.11}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-82952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97b5954bff8b5541ae3978022ac0c3d32b2258d67c2a190b4d128a4cfbbe4760"} {"question" "q-82952" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-82952" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{23.58}^{∘}"] ["math" "{37.33}^{∘}"] ["math" "{40.20}^{∘}"] ["math" "{49.11}^{∘}"] ["math" "{56.22}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-82952" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{49.11}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-82955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the acute angle between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-82955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x$ or any other non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 7+4\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 5}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{5}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{\\sqrt{38}\\sqrt{76}} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{38\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-82955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97b5954bff8b5541ae3978022ac0c3d32b2258d67c2a190b4d128a4cfbbe4760"} {"question" "q-82955" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-82955" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{3\\pi }{7}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-82955" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-140363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-140363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-140363" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97b5954bff8b5541ae3978022ac0c3d32b2258d67c2a190b4d128a4cfbbe4760"} {"question" "q-140363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-140363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-140363" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-140365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-140365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot (-1)}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{4\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4-3)^{2}+(0-2)^{2}+(2-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059805/state.json" "file_sha256" "fa4a893a53883d0245cee368f7dcbdf9964ba1885e70bfd6b3ef33891fa3c4f4"} {"question" "q-140365" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :